\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0260}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0260}}


Let $F$, $F'$ be algebraic spaces over $S$.
A {\it morphism $f : F \to F'$ of algebraic spaces over $S$}
is a transformation of functors from $F$ to $F'$.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02Z3}{\texttt{Stacks tag 02Z3}}



Notation $U \to S$, $G$, $R$ as in Lemma \ref{spaces-lemma-quotient}.
If the action of $G$ on $U$ satisfies $(*)$ we say $G$ {\it acts freely}
on the scheme $U$. In this case the algebraic space $U/R$ is denoted
$U/G$ and is called the {\it quotient of $U$ by $G$}.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02Z4}{\texttt{Stacks tag 02Z4}}



Notation and assumptions as in Lemma \ref{spaces-lemma-quotient}.
Assume $G$ is finite. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item if $U \to S$ is quasi-separated, then $U/G$ is quasi-separated
over $S$, and
\item if $U \to S$ is separated, then $U/G$ is separated over $S$.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02Z5}{\texttt{Stacks tag 02Z5}}



Notation and assumptions as in Lemma \ref{spaces-lemma-quotient}.
If $\Spec(k) \to U/G$ is a morphism, then there exist
\begin{enumerate}
\item a finite Galois extension $k'/k$,
\item a finite subgroup $H \subset G$,
\item an isomorphism $H \to \text{Gal}(k'/k)$, and
\item an $H$-equivariant morphism $\Spec(k') \to U$.
\end{enumerate}
Conversely, such data determine a morphism $\Spec(k) \to U/G$.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04SG}{\texttt{Stacks tag 04SG}}


Let $\Sch_{fppf}$ be a big fppf site.
Let $S \to S'$ be a morphism of this site.
The construction above give an equivalence of
categories
$$
\left\{
\begin{matrix}
\text{category of algebraic}\\
\text{spaces over }S
\end{matrix}
\right\}
\leftrightarrow
\left\{
\begin{matrix}
\text{category of pairs }(F', F' \to S)\text{ consisting}\\
\text{of an algebraic space }F'\text{ over }S'\text{ and a}\\
\text{morphism }F' \to S\text{ of algebraic spaces over }S'
\end{matrix}
\right\}
$$

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04SH}{\texttt{Stacks tag 04SH}}


Let $\Sch_{fppf}$ be a big fppf site.
Let $S \to S'$ be a morphism of this site.
Let $F'$ be a sheaf on $(\Sch/S')_{fppf}$.
The following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The restriction $F'|_{(\Sch/S)_{fppf}}$
is an algebraic space over $S$, and
\item the sheaf $h_S \times F'$ is an algebraic space over $S'$.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AD4}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0AD4}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. We say an algebraic space $X$ over $S$ is
{\it integral} if it is reduced, decent, and $|X|$ is irreducible.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0ENE}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0ENE}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be an integral algebraic space over $S$.
The {\it function field}, or the {\it field of rational functions}
of $X$ is the field $R(X)$ of
Lemma \ref{spaces-over-fields-lemma-integral-algebraic-space-rational-functions}.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BH2}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0BH2}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be an integral algebraic space over $S$.
Then $\Gamma(X, \mathcal{O}_X)$ is a domain.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BH3}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0BH3}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be a normal integral algebraic space over $S$.
Then $\Gamma(X, \mathcal{O}_X)$ is a normal domain.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0ENF}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0ENF}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be a decent algebraic space over $S$.
There are canonical bijections between the following sets:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the set of points of $X$, i.e., $|X|$,
\item the set of irreducible closed subsets of $|X|$,
\item the set of integral closed subspaces of $X$.
\end{enumerate}
The bijection from (1) to (2) sends $x$ to $\overline{\{x\}}$.
The bijection from (3) to (2) sends $Z$ to $|Z|$.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06LZ}{\texttt{Stacks tag 06LZ}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $S$.
Assume $X$ satisfies at least one of the following conditions
\begin{enumerate}
\item $X$ is quasi-separated and $\dim(X) = 0$,
\item $X$ is locally of finite type over a field $k$ and $\dim(X) = 0$,
\item $X$ is Noetherian and $\dim(X) = 0$, or
\item add more here.
\end{enumerate}
Then $X$ is a separated scheme and any quasi-compact open of $X$ is affine.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0ADC}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0ADC}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be a quasi-separated algebraic space over $S$.
Let $x \in |X|$. The following are equivalent
\begin{enumerate}
\item $x$ is a point of codimension $0$ on $X$,
\item the local ring of $X$ at $x$ has dimension $0$, and
\item $x$ is a generic point of an irreducible component of $|X|$.
\end{enumerate}
If true, then there exists an open subspace of $X$
containing $x$ which is a scheme.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B84}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0B84}}


Let $k$ be a field with algebraic closure $\overline{k}$.
Let $X$ be a quasi-separated algebraic space over $k$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If there exists a field extension $K/k$ such that
$X_K$ is a scheme, then $X_{\overline{k}}$ is a scheme.
\item If $X$ is quasi-compact and there exists a field extension
$K/k$ such that $X_K$ is a scheme, then $X_{k'}$
is a scheme for some finite separable extension $k'$ of $k$.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B87}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0B87}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $S$ and
let $G$ be a finite group acting freely on $X$. Set $Y = X/G$ as
in Properties of Spaces, Lemma \ref{spaces-properties-lemma-quotient}.
For $y \in |Y|$ the following are equivalent
\begin{enumerate}
\item $y$ is in the schematic locus of $Y$, and
\item there exists an affine open $U \subset X$
containing the preimage of $y$.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B88}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0B88}}


Let $k$ be a field with algebraic closure $\overline{k}$. Let $X$
be an algebraic space over $k$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item $X$ is decent and locally of finite type over $k$,
\item $X_{\overline{k}}$ is a scheme, and
\item any finite set of $\overline{k}$-rational points of $X_{\overline{k}}$
is contained in an affine.
\end{enumerate}
Then $X$ is a scheme.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06S1}{\texttt{Stacks tag 06S1}}


Let $k$ be a field. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a monomorphism of algebraic spaces
over $k$. If $Y$ is locally quasi-finite over $k$ so is $X$.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0DMQ}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0DMQ}}


Let $k$ be a field.
Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $k$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Let $x \in |X|$ be a point. We say $X$ is
{\it geometrically reduced at $x$} if $\mathcal{O}_{X, \overline{x}}$
is geometrically reduced over $k$.
\item We say $X$ is {\it geometrically reduced} over $k$
if $X$ is geometrically reduced at every point of $X$.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0DMR}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0DMR}}


Let $k$ be a field. Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $k$.
Let $x \in |X|$. The following are equivalent
\begin{enumerate}
\item $X$ is geometrically reduced at $x$,
\item for some \'etale neighbourhood $(U, u) \to (X, x)$
where $U$ is a scheme, $U$ is geometrically reduced at $u$,
\item for any \'etale neighbourhood $(U, u) \to (X, x)$
where $U$ is a scheme, $U$ is geometrically reduced at $u$.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0DMS}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0DMS}}


Let $k$ be a field. Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $k$.
The following are equivalent
\begin{enumerate}
\item $X$ is geometrically reduced,
\item for some surjective \'etale morphism $U \to X$ where $U$
is a scheme, $U$ is geometrically reduced,
\item for any \'etale morphism $U \to X$
where $U$ is a scheme, $U$ is geometrically reduced.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0E02}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0E02}}


Let $X$ be an algebraic space over a perfect field $k$ (for example
$k$ has characteristic zero).
\begin{enumerate}
\item For $x \in |X|$, if $\mathcal{O}_{X, \overline{x}}$ is
reduced, then $X$ is geometrically reduced at $x$.
\item If $X$ is reduced, then $X$ is geometrically reduced over $k$.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A17}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0A17}}


Let $k$ be a field. Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $k$.
The following are equivalent
\begin{enumerate}
\item $X$ is geometrically connected,
\item for every finite separable field extension $k'/k$
the algebraic space $X_{k'}$ is connected.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EDG}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0EDG}}


In the situation of Definition \ref{spaces-over-fields-definition-intersection-number}
the intersection number
$(\mathcal{L}_1 \cdots \mathcal{L}_d \cdot Z)$
is an integer.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EDI}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0EDI}}


In the situation of Definition \ref{spaces-over-fields-definition-intersection-number}
let $Z_i \subset Z$ be the irreducible components of dimension $d$. Let
$m_i = \text{length}_{\mathcal{O}_{X, \overline{x}_i}}
(\mathcal{O}_{Z, \overline{x}_i})$
where $\overline{x}_i$ is a geometric generic point of $Z_i$. Then
$$
(\mathcal{L}_1 \cdots \mathcal{L}_d \cdot Z) =
\sum m_i(\mathcal{L}_1 \cdots \mathcal{L}_d \cdot Z_i)
$$

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AMN}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0AMN}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $a : F \to G$ and $b : G \to H$ be
transformations of functors $(\Sch/S)_{fppf}^{opp} \to \textit{Sets}$.
Assume
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\Delta : G \to G \times_H G$ is representable
by algebraic spaces, and
\item $b \circ a : F \to H$ is representable by algebraic spaces.
\end{enumerate}
Then $a$ is representable by algebraic spaces.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07WE}{\texttt{Stacks tag 07WE}}


Let $S \in \Ob(\Sch_{fppf})$. Let $F$ be a presheaf of sets on
$(\Sch/S)_{fppf}$. Assume
\begin{enumerate}
\item $F$ is a sheaf for the Zariski topology on $(\Sch/S)_{fppf}$,
\item there exists an index set $I$ and subfunctors $F_i \subset F$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item each $F_i$ is an fppf sheaf,
\item each $F_i \to F$ is representable by algebraic spaces,
\item $\coprod F_i \to F$ becomes surjective after fppf sheafification.
\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}
Then $F$ is an fppf sheaf.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0DK3}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0DK3}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a finite morphism of algebraic
spaces over $S$. Let $\overline{y}$ be a geometric point of $Y$ with
lifts $\overline{x}_1, \ldots, \overline{x}_n$ in $X$. Then
$$
(f_*\mathcal{F})_{\overline{y}} =
\prod\nolimits_{i = 1, \ldots, n}
\mathcal{F}_{\overline{x}_i}
$$
for any sheaf $\mathcal{F}$ on $X_\etale$.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0722}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0722}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f_i : U_i \to X$ be \'etale morphisms
of algebraic spaces over $S$. Then there are isomorphisms
$$
f_{1, !}\underline{\mathbf{Z}} \otimes_{\mathbf{Z}}
f_{2, !}\underline{\mathbf{Z}}
\longrightarrow
f_{12, !}\underline{\mathbf{Z}}
$$
where $f_{12} : U_1 \times_X U_2 \to X$ is the structure morphism
and
$$
(f_1 \amalg f_2)_! \underline{\mathbf{Z}}
\longrightarrow
f_{1, !}\underline{\mathbf{Z}} \oplus
f_{2, !}\underline{\mathbf{Z}}
$$

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A4R}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0A4R}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be a quasi-compact and quasi-separated
algebraic space over $S$. Assume $\dim(X) \leq d$ for some integer $d$.
Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a quasi-coherent sheaf $\mathcal{F}$ on $X$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $H^q(X, \mathcal{F}) = 0$ for $q > d$,
\item $H^d(X, \mathcal{F}) \to H^d(U, \mathcal{F})$ is surjective
for any quasi-compact open $U \subset X$,
\item $H^q_Z(X, \mathcal{F}) = 0$ for $q > d$ for any closed subspace
$Z \subset X$ whose complement is quasi-compact.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07UA}{\texttt{Stacks tag 07UA}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be a locally Noetherian algebraic space over $S$.
A quasi-coherent module $\mathcal{F}$ on $X$ is called {\it coherent}
if $\mathcal{F}$ is a coherent $\mathcal{O}_X$-module on the site
$X_\etale$ in the sense of
Modules on Sites, Definition \ref{sites-modules-definition-site-local}.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07V0}{\texttt{Stacks tag 07V0}}


In Situation \ref{spaces-cohomology-situation-vanishing}.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Given an affine morphism $X' \to X$ of algebraic spaces, we have
$H^1(X', \mathcal{F}') = 0$ for every quasi-coherent
$\mathcal{O}_{X'}$-module $\mathcal{F}'$.
\item Given an $A$-algebra $A'$ setting $X' = X \times_{\Spec(A)} \Spec(A')$
the morphism $X' \to X$ is affine and $\Gamma(X', \mathcal{O}_{X'}) = A'$.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07V4}{\texttt{Stacks tag 07V4}}


In Situation \ref{spaces-cohomology-situation-vanishing} the morphism $p : X \to \Spec(A)$ is
universally injective.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07V5}{\texttt{Stacks tag 07V5}}


In Situation \ref{spaces-cohomology-situation-vanishing} the morphism $p : X \to \Spec(A)$ is
separated.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07VP}{\texttt{Stacks tag 07VP}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : Y \to X$ be a morphism of algebraic
spaces over $S$. Assume
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f$ finite,
\item $f$ surjective,
\item $Y$ affine, and
\item $X$ Noetherian.
\end{enumerate}
Then $X$ is affine.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GFB}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0GFB}}


Let $R$ be a Noetherian ring. Let $f : Y \to X$ be a morphism of
algebraic spaces proper over $R$. Let $\mathcal{L}$ be an
invertible $\mathcal{O}_X$-module. Assume $f$ is finite and surjective.
The following are equivalent
\begin{enumerate}
\item $X$ is a scheme and $\mathcal{L}$ is ample, and
\item $Y$ is a scheme and $f^*\mathcal{L}$ is ample.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03JT}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03JT}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $S$.
The following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item there exist schemes $U_i$ and \'etale morphisms
$U_i \to X$ such that $\coprod U_i \to X$ is surjective and
each $U_i \to X$ has universally bounded fibres, and
\item for every affine scheme $U$ and \'etale morphism $\varphi : U \to X$
the fibres of $U \to X$ are universally bounded.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03JX}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03JX}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $S$.
Consider the following conditions on $X$:
\begin{itemize}
\item[] $(\alpha)$ For every $x \in |X|$, the equivalent conditions of
Lemma \ref{decent-spaces-lemma-U-finite-above-x}
hold.
\item[] $(\beta)$ For every $x \in |X|$, the equivalent conditions of
Lemma \ref{decent-spaces-lemma-R-finite-above-x}
hold.
\item[] $(\gamma)$ For every $x \in |X|$, the equivalent conditions of
Lemma \ref{decent-spaces-lemma-UR-finite-above-x}
hold.
\item[] $(\delta)$ The equivalent conditions of
Lemma \ref{decent-spaces-lemma-U-universally-bounded}
hold.
\item[] $(\epsilon)$ The equivalent conditions of
Lemma \ref{decent-spaces-lemma-characterize-very-reasonable}
hold.
\item[] $(\zeta)$ The space $X$ is Zariski locally quasi-separated.
\item[] $(\eta)$ The space $X$ is quasi-separated
\item[] $(\theta)$ The space $X$ is representable, i.e., $X$ is a scheme.
\item[] $(\iota)$ The space $X$ is a quasi-separated scheme.
\end{itemize}
We have
$$
\begin{array}{ccccccc} & (\theta) \searrow &  &  &  &  &  \\ (\iota) \nearrow\searrow &  & (\zeta) \Longrightarrow & (\epsilon) \Longrightarrow & (\delta) \Longrightarrow & (\gamma) \Longleftrightarrow & (\alpha) + (\beta) \\  & (\eta) \nearrow &  &  &  &  & \end{array}
$$

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03I8}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03I8}}


Let $S$ be a scheme.
Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $S$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item We say $X$ is {\it decent} if for every point $x \in X$ the equivalent
conditions of
Lemma \ref{decent-spaces-lemma-UR-finite-above-x}
hold, in other words property $(\gamma)$ of
Lemma \ref{decent-spaces-lemma-bounded-fibres}
holds.
\item We say $X$ is {\it reasonable} if the equivalent conditions of
Lemma \ref{decent-spaces-lemma-U-universally-bounded}
hold, in other words property $(\delta)$ of
Lemma \ref{decent-spaces-lemma-bounded-fibres}
holds.
\item We say $X$ is {\it very reasonable} if the equivalent conditions of
Lemma \ref{decent-spaces-lemma-characterize-very-reasonable}
hold, i.e., property $(\epsilon)$ of
Lemma \ref{decent-spaces-lemma-bounded-fibres}
holds.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09YB}{\texttt{Stacks tag 09YB}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be a quasi-compact and quasi-separated
algebraic space over $S$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item There exists a surjective integral morphism $Y \to X$ where $Y$
is a scheme,
\item given a surjective \'etale morphism $U \to X$ we may choose
$Y \to X$ such that for every $y \in Y$ there is an open neighbourhood
$V \subset Y$ such that $V \to X$ factors through $U$.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BA1}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0BA1}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X \to Y$ be a surjective finite locally free
morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$. For $y \in |Y|$ the following are
equivalent
\begin{enumerate}
\item $y$ is in the schematic locus of $Y$, and
\item there exists an affine open $U \subset X$
containing the preimage of $y$.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EMW}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0EMW}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be a decent algebraic space over $S$.
Let $x \in |X|$. The {\it residue field of $X$ at $x$}
is the unique field $\kappa(x)$ which comes equipped with a
monomorphism $\Spec(\kappa(x)) \to X$ representing $x$.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EMX}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0EMX}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of decent
algebraic spaces over $S$. Let $x \in |X|$ be a point
with image $y = f(x) \in |Y|$.
The following are equivalent
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f$ induces an isomorphism $\kappa(y) \to \kappa(x)$, and
\item the induced morphism $\Spec(\kappa(x)) \to Y$ is a monomorphism.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BGU}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0BGU}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $S$.
Let $x \in X$ be a point. An {\it elementary \'etale neighbourhood}
is an \'etale morphism $(U, u) \to (X, x)$ where $U$ is a scheme,
$u \in U$ is a point mapping to $x$, and the morphism
$u = \Spec(\kappa(u)) \to X$ is a monomorphism.
A {\it morphism of elementary \'etale neighbourhoods}
$(U, u) \to (U', u')$ is defined as a morphism $U \to U'$
over $X$ mapping $u$ to $u'$.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A4J}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0A4J}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Dimension as defined in
Properties of Spaces, Section \ref{spaces-properties-section-dimension}
behaves well on decent algebraic spaces $X$ over $S$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $x \in |X|$, then $\dim_x(|X|) = \dim_x(X)$, and
\item $\dim(|X|) = \dim(X)$.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0ED0}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0ED0}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X \to Y$ be a locally quasi-finite morphism
of algebraic spaces over $S$. Then $\dim(X) \leq \dim(Y)$.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06QW}{\texttt{Stacks tag 06QW}}


Let $S$ be a scheme.
Let $Z$ be an algebraic space over $S$. The following are equivalent
\begin{enumerate}
\item $Z$ is reduced and $|Z|$ is a singleton,
\item there exists a surjective flat morphism $\Spec(k) \to Z$
where $k$ is a field, and
\item there exists a locally of finite type, surjective, flat morphism
$\Spec(k) \to Z$ where $k$ is a field.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06QX}{\texttt{Stacks tag 06QX}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $Z$ be an algebraic space over $S$.
The following are equivalent
\begin{enumerate}
\item $Z$ is reduced, locally Noetherian, and $|Z|$
is a singleton, and
\item there exists a locally finitely presented, surjective, flat morphism
$\Spec(k) \to Z$ where $k$ is a field.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0H1S}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0H1S}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : Y \to X$ be a morphism of algebraic
spaces over $S$. Let $x \in |X|$ be a point. Assume
\begin{enumerate}
\item $|f|(|Y|)$ is contained in $\{x\} \subset |X|$,
\item $Y$ is reduced, and
\item $X$ is locally Noetherian.
\end{enumerate}
Then $f$ factors through the residual space $Z_x$ of $X$ at $x$.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/047Z}{\texttt{Stacks tag 047Z}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be a decent algebraic space over $S$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $|X|$ is a singleton then $X$ is a scheme.
\item If $|X|$ is a singleton and $X$ is reduced, then
$X \cong \Spec(k)$ for some field $k$.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03L0}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03L0}}


Having property $(\beta)$, being decent, or being reasonable
is preserved under arbitrary base change.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03L1}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03L1}}


Having property $(\beta)$, being decent, or being reasonable
is preserved under compositions.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AC6}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0AC6}}


In diagram (\ref{equation-points-fibres}) the set (\ref{equation-fibre})
