\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06C4}{\texttt{Stacks tag 06C4}}


Let $S$ be a scheme.
Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item We say $f$ is a {\it Koszul morphism}, or that $f$ is a
{\it local complete intersection morphism} if the equivalent conditions of
Morphisms of Spaces, Lemma \ref{spaces-morphisms-lemma-local-source-target}
hold with $\mathcal{P}(f) =$``$f$ is a local complete intersection morphism''.
\item Let $x \in |X|$. We say $f$ is {\it Koszul at $x$} if
there exists an open neighbourhood $X' \subset X$ of $x$ such
that $f|_{X'} : X' \to Y$ is a local complete intersection morphism.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06C6}{\texttt{Stacks tag 06C6}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a local complete intersection
morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f$ is locally of finite presentation,
\item $f$ is pseudo-coherent, and
\item $f$ is perfect.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06C7}{\texttt{Stacks tag 06C7}}


A flat base change of a local complete intersection morphism is a
local complete intersection morphism.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06C8}{\texttt{Stacks tag 06C8}}


A composition of local complete intersection morphisms is a
local complete intersection morphism.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06C9}{\texttt{Stacks tag 06C9}}



Let $S$ be a scheme.
Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$.
The following are equivalent
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f$ is flat and a local complete intersection morphism, and
\item $f$ is syntomic.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CHK}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0CHK}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. A Koszul-regular immersion of algebraic spaces
over $S$ is a local complete intersection morphism.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CHM}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0CHM}}


The property $\mathcal{P}(f) =$``$f$ is a local complete intersection
morphism'' is fpqc local on the base.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CHN}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0CHN}}


The property $\mathcal{P}(f) =$``$f$ is a local complete intersection
morphism'' is syntomic local on the source.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0DSE}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0DSE}}


Let $S$ be a scheme.
Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$.
We say $f$ is {\it at-worst-nodal of relative dimension $1$}
if the equivalent conditions of
Morphisms of Spaces, Lemma \ref{spaces-morphisms-lemma-local-source-target}
hold with $\mathcal{P} =$``at-worst-nodal of relative dimension $1$''.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0DSF}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0DSF}}


The property of being at-worst-nodal of relative dimension $1$
is preserved under base change.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03HL}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03HL}}


Let $S$ be a scheme.
Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$.
Let $\Delta_{X/Y} : X \to X \times_Y X$ be the diagonal morphism.
\begin{enumerate}
\item We say $f$ is {\it separated} if $\Delta_{X/Y}$ is a closed immersion.
\item We say $f$ is {\it locally separated}\footnote{In the literature
this term often refers to quasi-separated and locally separated morphisms.}
if $\Delta_{X/Y}$ is an immersion.
\item We say $f$ is {\it quasi-separated} if $\Delta_{X/Y}$ is quasi-compact.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03KL}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03KL}}


All of the separation axioms listed in Definition \ref{spaces-morphisms-definition-separated}
are stable under base change.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03KQ}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03KQ}}


All of the separation axioms listed in Definition \ref{spaces-morphisms-definition-separated}
are stable under composition of morphisms.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03KR}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03KR}}


Let $S$ be a scheme.
Let $f : X \to Y$ and $g : Y \to Z$ be morphisms of algebraic spaces over $S$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $g \circ f$ is separated then so is $f$.
\item If $g \circ f$ is locally separated then so is $f$.
\item If $g \circ f$ is quasi-separated then so is $f$.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04ZI}{\texttt{Stacks tag 04ZI}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $S$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $X$ is separated then $X$ is separated over $S$.
\item If $X$ is locally separated then $X$ is locally separated over $S$.
\item If $X$ is quasi-separated then $X$ is quasi-separated over $S$.
\end{enumerate}
Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(4)] If $X$ is separated over $S$ then $f$ is separated.
\item[(5)] If $X$ is locally separated over $S$ then $f$ is locally separated.
\item[(6)] If $X$ is quasi-separated over $S$ then $f$ is quasi-separated.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03KY}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03KY}}


Let $S$ be a scheme.
Let $f : X \to Y$ be a representable morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The morphism $f$ is locally separated.
\item The morphism $f$ is (quasi-)separated in the sense of
Definition \ref{spaces-morphisms-definition-separated}
above if and only if $f$ is (quasi-)separated in the sense of
Section \ref{spaces-morphisms-section-representable}.
\end{enumerate}
In particular, if $f : X \to Y$ is a morphism of schemes over $S$, then
$f$ is (quasi-)separated in the sense of
Definition \ref{spaces-morphisms-definition-separated}
if and only if $f$ is (quasi-)separated as a morphism of schemes.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03ME}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03ME}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of algebraic
spaces over $S$. We say $f$ is {\it surjective}
if the map $|f| : |X| \to |Y|$ of associated topological spaces
is surjective.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03Z1}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03Z1}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a representable morphism of
algebraic spaces over $S$. The following are equivalent
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f$ is universally open
(in the sense of Section \ref{spaces-morphisms-section-representable}), and
\item for every morphism of algebraic spaces $Z \to Y$ the morphism of
topological spaces $|Z \times_Y X| \to |Z|$ is open.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03Z3}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03Z3}}


The base change of a universally open morphism of algebraic spaces
by any morphism of algebraic spaces is universally open.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03Z4}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03Z4}}


The composition of a pair of (universally) open morphisms of algebraic spaces
is (universally) open.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CFQ}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0CFQ}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a representable morphism of
algebraic spaces over $S$. The following are equivalent
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f$ is universally submersive
(in the sense of Section \ref{spaces-morphisms-section-representable}), and
\item for every morphism of algebraic spaces $Z \to Y$ the morphism of
topological spaces $|Z \times_Y X| \to |Z|$ is submersive.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0412}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0412}}


Let $S$ be a scheme.
Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item We say $f$ is {\it submersive}\footnote{This is very different
from the notion of a submersion of differential manifolds.}
if the continuous map $|X| \to |Y|$ is submersive, see
Topology, Definition \ref{topology-definition-submersive}.
\item We say $f$ is {\it universally submersive} if for every
morphism of algebraic spaces $Y' \to Y$ the base change
$Y' \times_Y X \to Y'$ is submersive.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CFR}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0CFR}}


The base change of a universally submersive morphism of algebraic spaces
by any morphism of algebraic spaces is universally submersive.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CFS}{\texttt{Stacks tag 0CFS}}


The composition of a pair of (universally) submersive morphisms of
algebraic spaces is (universally) submersive.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03HD}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03HD}}


Let $S$ be a scheme.
Let $f : X \to Y$ be a representable morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$.
The following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f$ is quasi-compact
(in the sense of Section \ref{spaces-morphisms-section-representable}), and
\item for every quasi-compact algebraic space $Z$ and any morphism
$Z \to Y$ the algebraic space $Z \times_Y X$ is quasi-compact.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03HF}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03HF}}


The base change of a quasi-compact morphism of algebraic spaces
by any morphism of algebraic spaces is quasi-compact.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03HG}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03HG}}


The composition of a pair of quasi-compact morphisms of algebraic spaces
is quasi-compact.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/040W}{\texttt{Stacks tag 040W}}



Let $S$ be a scheme.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $X \to Y$ is a surjective morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$,
and $X$ is quasi-compact then $Y$ is quasi-compact.
\item If
$$
\begin{array}{ccc}X \underset{f}{\longrightarrow}\underset{p}{\searrow} &  & Y \overset{q}{\swarrow} \\  & Z & \end{array}
$$
is a commutative diagram of morphisms of algebraic spaces over $S$
and $f$ is surjective and $p$ is quasi-compact, then $q$ is quasi-compact.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03XD}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03XD}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a representable morphism of
algebraic spaces over $S$. The following are equivalent
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f$ is universally closed
(in the sense of Section \ref{spaces-morphisms-section-representable}), and
\item for every morphism of algebraic spaces $Z \to Y$ the morphism of
topological spaces $|Z \times_Y X| \to |Z|$ is closed.
\end{enumerate}

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03IS}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03IS}}


The base change of a universally closed morphism of algebraic spaces
by any morphism of algebraic spaces is universally closed.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03IU}{\texttt{Stacks tag 03IU}}


The composition of a pair of (universally) closed morphisms of algebraic spaces
is (universally) closed.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04XW}{\texttt{Stacks tag 04XW}}


Let $S$ be a scheme.
A universally closed morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$ is quasi-compact.

\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05Z2}{\texttt{Stacks tag 05Z2}}


Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $B$ be an algebraic space over $S$.
Let $f : X \to Y$ be a surjective universally closed
morphism of algebraic spaces over $B$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $X$ is quasi-separated, then $Y$ is quasi-separated.
\item If $X$ is separated, then $Y$ is separated.
\item If $X$ is quasi-separated over $B$, then $Y$ is quasi-separated over $B$.
\item If $X$ is separated over $B$, then $Y$ is separated over $B$.
